From 97db4b8d4cf0da6f03cdb42f53b327a829662fb8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Dohmke <thomas@dohmke.de>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] Add a fixed-kernel mode for directional soft shadows

New project setting rendering/lights_and_shadows/directional_shadow/soft_shadow_filter_kernel:
0 Rotated (default, stock), 1 Fixed, 2 Fixed With Early Out, 3 Fixed With Runtime Loop. Read by
RenderingServer.directional_soft_shadow_filter_set_quality(), so it can change at run time.

The stock soft PCF (scene_forward_lights_inc.glsl, sample_directional_pcf_shadow) rotates a Vogel disk per
pixel: an interleaved-gradient hash of gl_FragCoord, sin and cos, a mat2, and per tap a rotated offset
built from a scene UBO kernel entry. It is inlined into the directional light loop of the forward
fragment shader, after the material code, while every material output is still live, and its loop runs
to a specialization constant, so the driver unrolls it. Measured on an M2 (Marvin's port at 1080p): the
soft filter costs ~4 ms more than the hard filter (one bilinear comparison), and 8 taps (Soft Medium) were
not measurably cheaper than 16 (Soft High) on the frozen town view (within its +-0.6 ms run spread).
Suspected: the soft path's extra live state (lower occupancy of an already register-heavy shader), plus
taps multiplied by transparent overdraw (to be confirmed with a GPU capture).

The fixed modes take the same taps for every pixel: rings of four point-symmetric taps at equal-area radii
(get_fixed_ring_disk, outermost ring first, centroid exactly zero so edges do not shift), each a hardware
bilinear depth comparison, scaled by the light's blur and the quality radius as before. No hash, no
sin/cos, no rotation matrix, no noise; the sample count still comes from soft_shadow_filter_quality. This
is the classic fixed-kernel PCF, and what SceneKit's own ComputeSoftShadow does (shadowSampleCount taps of
a fixed shadowKernel x shadowRadius, one sample_compare each). Early out takes the outer ring first and
stops when its four taps agree (fully lit or fully shadowed). Runtime loop gives the same image but reads
the sample count from the kernel (the first tap's w) rather than the specialization constant, so the
compiler cannot unroll the loop, as in SceneKit, whose sample count is a uniform: fewer taps in flight,
fewer live registers. SPIRV-Cross drops [[dont_unroll]] for Metal, so this is the only way to keep the
loop rolled there. Modes 1-3 are meant to be measured against each other.

The mode is a 2-bit specialization constant (packed_1 bits 6-7) in the Forward+ scene shader, so the
unused paths compile out. Renderers without the fixed path (Mobile) keep the rotated Vogel disk
(_has_fixed_directional_soft_shadow_kernel()).

